<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; The Sources of Prayer" cic="CCC 2590-2649" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class or in a group. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What are the main sources of Christian prayer?</li>
<li>What is Lectio Divina and what are its four steps?</li>
<li>What place does the Liturgy have in the life of prayer of the Church?</li>
<li>What does &laquo;praying without ceasing&raquo; mean according to the Catechism?</li>
<li>What is the &laquo;Shema Israel&raquo; and what does it teach about prayer?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question honestly.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. Do you read the Bible regularly? If not, what prevents you? If yes, how do you read it?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">It is not about how much you read, but how you read it. Do you read it like any other book or as the Word of God?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. What is your favorite psalm? Read it and write why you like it.</p>
<p class="question-block__p">If you do not have one, browse the book of Psalms and find one that speaks to your heart today.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. How do you experience the Mass? Do you participate actively or attend out of habit?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">The Mass is the source and summit of Christian prayer. What could you do to live it better?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. What ordinary activity of your day could you &laquo;turn into prayer&raquo; by offering it to God?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Think of a concrete action: driving, cooking, studying, working. How would it change if you did it for love of God?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. Of the sources of prayer (Bible, Tradition, Liturgy, daily life), which one do you want to cultivate most this week?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Choose one. Make a concrete plan: &laquo;I will read 5 minutes of the Bible each day&raquo; or &laquo;I will pray the Angelus at noon.&raquo;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. Practice Lectio Divina with a short passage</h4>
<p>Choose John 15:1-8 (the vine and the branches). Read the passage slowly. Meditate: what is Jesus saying to you? Pray: respond to him. Contemplate: sit in silence for a few minutes. Write what you discovered.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>B. Pray a psalm each day for a week</h4>
<p>Choose 7 psalms, one for each day. You can use this selection: Day 1: Ps 1; Day 2: Ps 23; Day 3: Ps 27; Day 4: Ps 51; Day 5: Ps 63; Day 6: Ps 100; Day 7: Ps 150. Read them aloud. Mark the verses that speak to you most.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>C. Participate more consciously in the Mass</h4>
<p>At your next Mass, arrive 5 minutes early. Read the day's readings before the celebration begins. During Mass, follow each part attentively. Afterwards, write one phrase that resonated with you.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Memory Verse</h3>

<div class="bible-passage">
<p>&laquo;Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path.&raquo;</p>
<p class="bible-passage__ref">&mdash; Psalm 119:105</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Prayer for This Week</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Lord, teach me to drink from the sources of prayer.<br />
Open my heart to your Word,<br />
that it may not be dead letters but a living voice.<br />
Give me love for the liturgy,<br />
that in every Mass I may encounter you.<br />
Teach me through the example of the saints,<br />
who knew how to make their lives unceasing prayer.<br />
May everything I do today be for your glory.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

